 "result": "\n\nSaved `scenario.json` for `G4-CAL-05`.\n\n**Request:** \"Please change the display name of the calendar entry that's currently showing in my list as 'APAC team' — the one I have write access to but don't own — to 'APAC team 2018'.\" Target is Jordan's list entry for `apac@northwind.example` (Kenji's \"APAC\" calendar, overridden display name \"APAC team\", Jordan a writer); the action is `PATCH /users/me/calendarList/...` setting `summaryOverride`.\n\n**Decoys:**\n- `apac-notes@` — **A:CalendarListEntry.access_role**, F7: Jordan's *own* calendar displayed as \"APAC team\" and visible, but his role is `owner`, the level above `writer` on the role scale (fails c3).\n- `apac-team@` — **A:CalendarListEntry.summary_override**, F1: the calendar's own summary is exactly \"APAC team\" (the designated substitute `Calendar.summary`), but the entry's display override is \"APAC\" (fails c2).\n- `apac-team-old@` — **A:CalendarListEntry.summary_override**, F8: a visible writer calendar whose display name merely contains the requested one, \"APAC team (old)\" (fails c2).\n- `apac-team-2017@` — **A:CalendarListEntry.hidden**, F0: displayed as \"APAC team\" with Jordan as writer, but the entry is hidden, so it isn't the one currently showing (fails c1).\n\nBackground entries (`sales@`, `holidays@`, plus the pre-seeded primary) each fail at least two conditions. I skipped the ACL-based F1 decoy for `access_role` since the replica 403s non-owner ACL reads (rule 6), and traced every mutation: each mutated query selects its witness, the original selects only the target.",
